WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi, I'm Mike
Coleman, a developer advocate

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:03.930
at Google Cloud.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.810
In this video, I want
to introduce you to Kf.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.820
As your teams standardize
on Kubernetes,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.040
migrating applications
from existing platforms,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.520
like Cloud Foundry, is often
one of the biggest challenges.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.610
Kf was designed to help
teams minimize any disruption

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.040
to developer workflows during
the migration to Google

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.080
Kubernetes Engine and Anthos.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.990
Kf offers developers the
Cloud Foundry experience,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.870
while empowering platform
admins and operators

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.170
to adopt declarative
Kubernetes practices.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.180
It makes migrating
Cloud Foundry workloads

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.940
to Kubernetes straightforward,
and most importantly,

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.700
avoids major changes to
application developer

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.810
workflows.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.240
You can also eliminate
commercial Cloud Foundry

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.400
licensing costs
and take advantage

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.310
of the config and policy
features of Anthos and GKE

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.720
for governance.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.640
Before I show you a
demonstration of Kf,

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.560
I wanted to talk a bit
about the architecture

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.020
and how we map Cloud Foundry
primitives and services

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.280
to Kubernetes.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.150
Kf requires Kubernetes and other
open source projects to run.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.830
Some of the dependencies
are satisfied

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.360
with Google-managed services.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.760
For example, Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.860
provides the Kubernetes backend.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.810
In addition to
Kubernetes, Kf leverages

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.350
Tekton to enable Kf build,
which can handle both Buildpack

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.150
and Docker file builds.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.000
To take care of setting up
routes for applications,

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.340
Kf uses Anthos
Service Mesh, which

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.540
is built on Google's
Manage Istio offering.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.540
By default, Kf includes
native integration

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.820
with Google Cloud's Cloud
Monitoring and Cloud Logging

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:36.690
services.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.150
This integration lets you
monitor your running clusters,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.820
and help analyze your system
and application performance,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.270
using advanced profiling
and tracing capabilities.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.450
On the monitoring side, Kf has
both an application performance

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.040
dashboard, which
provides information

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.920
on metrics such as requests
per second, round trip

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.800
latency, HTTP error
codes, and more,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.290
as well as a systems
dashboard, which has data

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.320
on nodes, pods,
containers, et cetera.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.680
Kf installs several of its own
Kubernetes custom resources

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:05.820
and controllers.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.440
The customer
resources effectively

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.979
serve as the Kf API, and
are used by the Kf CLI

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:12.840
to interact with the system.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.780
The controllers use
KFCRDs to orchestrate

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.450
the other components
in the system.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.630
You can view these
resources using kubectl.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.580
Before we jump into
our demo, I want

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.580
to talk for just a minute
about service brokers.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.500
Service brokers are
software components

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.380
that provide an abstraction
of manage service life cycles

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.490
across different platforms.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.220
Kf supports service
brokers that are

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.790
built according to the open
service broker specifications.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.930
Currently, Kf only supports
credentialed services.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.480
Special services,
such as syslog drains,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.210
volume services, route
services, service keys,

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.180
and shared services
aren't yet supported.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.430
In the demo, I'll be
using the Cloud Service

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.680
Broker to provision a memory
store for Redis instance.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.487
On that note, let's
jump into the demo,

00:02:58.487 --> 00:03:00.570
where I'll show you how
to deploy the spring music

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.120
app to Kubernetes using
Kf, as well as how

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.330
to provision and manage a
Google Cloud based Redis service

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.640
as the backing data store.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.470
As we start our
demo, I've actually

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.560
already set up my
cluster-running Kf,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.180
so there are my nodes.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.740
And I've also already configured
the marketplace, or the Cloud

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.730
Service Broker.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.530
So we're going to use this Redis
service here later in the demo,

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.280
but I just want to
point it out right now.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.210
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.040
is create a space
to hold our app.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.400
So I'll create sm-space
for spring music space,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.220
and then we'll go ahead
and target that space.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.240
And now, if we do
a Kf spaces, you

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.130
can see the space
we just created,

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:47.180
right there listed sm-space.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.110
The other thing is if
we go in and we kubectl

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.510
and we say get spaces,
we can see the name space

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.070
for sm-space, so
that's going back

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.050
to where Kubernetes
primitives are being

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:57.935
mapped into Kf primitives.

00:03:57.935 --> 00:03:59.560
So let's go ahead
and push our app out,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.310
so we'll just do a Kf
push-- we're already

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:02.775
in the directory with the repo.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.900
And that's going to go
ahead and upload the source,

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:05.800
and going to compile it.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.360
It's going to take a little bit,
so I'll fast-forward the video,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.760
and I'll rejoin
you when it's done.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.320
With the application
deployed, let's go ahead

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:12.500
and visit it in the browser.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.333
So clear the screen.

00:04:13.333 --> 00:04:15.340
I'll do a Kf apps,
and that will show me

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.690
the route to the application.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.565
And I'm just going to go ahead
and grab that URL right here,

00:04:19.565 --> 00:04:21.190
and we're going to
copy that, and we're

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.560
going to paste it into a
new tab in our browser.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.920
So I'll move up here, create
a new tab, paste that in,

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.420
and there's our
application running.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.490
Now what I want to do is I want
to come over here and point out

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.650
that we're not using any
profiles or services.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.757
We're going to add
those in the next step.

00:04:34.757 --> 00:04:36.340
But I do want to add
a record so I can

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.632
have something in the database.

00:04:37.632 --> 00:04:39.730
So I had this Paul
McCartney record.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.500
I'll scroll down,
and you can see

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.060
that that has been written
to the local data store.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.352
So now what we're going to
do is we're going to go back

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:48.680
and we're going to
connect Redis into this.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.440
So let's move back
into the command line.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.120
I'm going to go ahead
and clear the screen,

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.200
and then what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.120
going to issue the
Create Service command,

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.660
and that's going to create that
Cloud Service-backed Google

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.880
Redis database in a
high availability mode,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.070
